8. Cyber Essentials Plus Test Specification v3.2

The CE Plus test specification is especially important because it reveals what assessors actually test when verifying controls.

8.1 Purpose and Audience

IASME states that the document is aimed primarily at assessors carrying out Cyber Essentials Plus assessments, although it may also be useful to applicants wishing to understand how compliance is judged. Its purpose is to enable independent testing of compliance with the technical requirements of the scheme and to define pass/fail criteria.

This is highly valuable to the present project because it allows the artefact to be informed not only by stated requirements, but by the actual verification logic behind those requirements.

8.2 Pre-Assessment Requirements

Before testing begins, the assessor must:

obtain appropriate written permission
verify that the scope matches the valid CE self-assessment certificate
confirm technically that the scope matches the networks and systems being assessed
verify effective segregation where the scope is not the whole organisation
identify the samples to be tested
ensure the correct report template is in place

IASME states that evidence relating to these preparatory checks must be retained by the certifying body for at least the lifetime of the certificate.

This highlights the importance of boundary definition, scope control, and evidence retention, which are useful design considerations for a readiness toolkit.

9. Pass/Fail Logic

The test specification states that each test case and sub-test is marked either pass or fail. All sub-tests within a test case must pass for the test case to pass, and all test cases must pass for the overall assessment to pass, unless a specific exception applies.

IASME also notes that advisory notes may accompany results in order to explain rationales or identify improvements.

Key Assessment Principle
Any single failed sub-test may lead to the parent test case, and therefore the overall assessment, being marked as failed unless an exception is explicitly permitted.

This is important because it illustrates the strict assurance logic applied in CE Plus and helps explain why SMEs may benefit from pre-assessment readiness support.

10. Test Case 1: Remote Vulnerability Assessment

This test case is intended to determine whether an internet-based opportunist attacker could compromise the applicant’s systems using common low-skill methods.

Key Requirements Extracted
All relevant IP addresses must be identified, including IaaS where applicable
All identified IPs must be scanned using an approved vulnerability scanning tool
Internet-facing services are then judged against a defined decision process
Main Decision Criteria Identified
Presence of vulnerabilities with CVSS 7.0 or above
Whether the service is public and read-only
Whether authentication is required
Whether multi-factor authentication is used
Whether default passwords have been changed
Whether login attempts are throttled
Whether lockout occurs after no more than 10 failed login attempts
Important Thresholds
CVSS ≥7.0 is treated as a key risk threshold
Maximum failed login attempts before lockout: 10

This is directly useful to the project because it shows where readiness tools can help organisations review internet-exposed services, change defaults, document authentication protections, and improve access hardening.

11. Representative Sample Testing

The specification states that for all but the smallest networks, it is impractical to test every device. Instead, assessors should use representative samples while ensuring sufficient confidence that the sample reflects all in-scope devices.

Sample Testing Rules Identified
Applies to end-user devices, internally hosted servers, and cloud services
Standardised configurations may reduce the number of required samples
Sample size calculation must be documented
Evidence of the sample calculation must be retained for the lifetime of the certificate
For cloud services, at least one normal user and one administrative user must be tested per service

This supports the logic of toolkit templates that help SMEs identify systems, user roles, and service types clearly.

12. Test Case 2: Authenticated Vulnerability Scan / Patch Management

This test is designed to identify missing vulnerability fixes on sampled end-user devices, servers, and IaaS instances.

Conditions That Trigger Concern

The assessor identifies vulnerabilities that meet any of the following:

vendor classifies them as critical or high risk
CVSS v3 base score of 7 or above
vendor does not specify severity details for the vulnerability fixed by the update
Key Rule

If a vulnerability fix has been available for more than 14 days and has not been applied, the sub-test is failed.

Additional Note

IASME states that virtual patching is not accepted as a long-term mitigation for legacy unsupported operating systems.

This is one of the most important findings for the project because it strongly supports the inclusion of patch-tracking templates, remediation guidance, and update governance within the artefact.

13. Test Case 3: Malware Protection

This test case applies to sampled end-user devices, servers with interactive desktops, and IaaS instances.

Acceptable Protection Approaches
anti-malware software
application allow listing
Sub-test Areas Identified
Email-delivered malware defence
Browser-downloaded malware defence
Manual checks to confirm anti-malware is installed, operational, and updated
Application allow listing controls, including trust and code execution restrictions
Operational Expectations
malicious files should not be accessible to users without controls intervening
executable files should not run without additional protective interaction
anti-malware logs and update state should demonstrate correct operation

This is relevant to the project because SMEs may assume that simply installing antivirus is sufficient, whereas IASME’s model shows that configuration, operation, and verification also matter.

14. Test Case 4: Multi-Factor Authentication

This test applies to all cloud services in scope.

Core Requirement

Users and administrators accessing cloud services must be prompted for multi-factor authentication when accessing from an untrusted device or incognito session.

Key Observations
all cloud services are in scope
both user and administrative access are tested
where several services share an authentication platform, testing can be applied per authentication service rather than separately for every application

This is one of the most significant practical findings from the source. For the Tanasiom Aegis project, it strongly justifies dedicated guidance on cloud MFA implementation and evidence capture.

15. Test Case 5: Account Separation

This test applies to sampled end-user devices, servers with interactive desktops, and cloud environments where administrative processes can be run.

Core Principle

Standard user accounts should not be able to perform administrative actions without separate privileged authentication.

Assessment Logic

A user logged in with a normal account attempts to perform an administrative process. If the action runs under the same account rather than requiring separate credentials, the sub-test is failed.

This directly supports the inclusion of access-control templates and guidance on separate administrator accounts within the artefact.

16. Concluding the Assessment

Once all testing is complete, the assessor compiles a report. If all test cases are passed, the applicant may be awarded Cyber Essentials Plus certification.

IASME also allows a limited exception process where failures result only from a small number of minor issues and there is no indication of broader process weakness.

Exception Criteria Identified
less than 5% of performed tests show only marginal deviation
there is no evidence of wider failure in the applicant’s cybersecurity processes
delivery partner discretion is required

This is useful for academic discussion because it shows that although the scheme is strict, there remains some limited room for professional judgement in borderline cases.

